  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \nThe goal is to design a sequence of non-negative coefficients that minimizes the autocorrelation peak under the sum constraint. The previous approach used multiple initialization methods and gradient-based optimization. Our new approach combines these with entropy optimization to balance the sequence and identify active lags more effectively, and then applies an affine scaling method to better converge to the minimum. We also target edge reductions in the local search by involving branch-and-bound from the initial setup to prevent premature simplification.  \n\n2. Mathematical specification  \nWe optimize the normalized entropy:  \n`objective(sequence) = 2 * n * max(convolve(a, a)) / (sum(a))^2`  \n*((matching the evaluation function)*).  \nWe use Lagrange multipliers to balance normalization, entropy, and activation patterns.  \nThe optimization splits the timeline between entropy-based and affine scaling-based phases, as well as coarse-grained and fine-grained structure exploration.  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \nAfter time estimation and gradient-based initialization with entropy balance, we proceed to affine scaling optimization for the second half. The code specifically:  \n- Uses FFT convolution initialization  \n- Employs entropy optimization through softmax normalization  \n- Fine-tunes via Adam gradient descent  \n- Includes a residues reconstruction and hybrid refinement phase  \n- Targets edge reductions independently  \n- Uses exact evaluation with `evaluate_sequence()`  \n- Balances time between phases  \nLet's implement accordingly.  \n\n4. Search choices and useful variations  \nWe consider four lag-related structurally different parent patterns (JNT6, JNT12, JNT18) and corresponding regularizations (L1 and L2). The active search work various parametrizations. The algorithm's core sequence matching leverages smooth iterative approaches with early stopping comparisons to prevent instability.  \n\n5. Compute budget and robustness  \nWe reserve 25 seconds for startup and validation constants.  \nFor sequences of no more than 512, we explore both short and long initializations.  \nFor very long sequences (100s to 1e6 elements), we focus on aggressive pruning and mutation hyperparameter experiments.  \nWe include exponential cooling schedules and multi-regularization hybrid methods to maintain feasibility.  \n\n6. Validation and return contract  \nCandidates are rigorously tested with `evaluate_sequence()` ensuring all updates involve normalization and gradient checks.  \nThe final return must normalize all by the evaluated sum.`1.0/(sum)**(2/3)`during initial minimization, then switch to affine scaling balance.`(1+\u025b)`for several micro-optimization cycles matching entropy value expectations To meet the performance metric.",
